# =============================================================================
# ███  PRODUCTION ENV — read this before touching anything  ███
# =============================================================================
#
# This is the template for the PRODUCTION secrets file. Copy it to
# `docker/.env` (same folder) and fill in real values:
#
#     cp docker/.env.production.example docker/.env
#
# The PROD compose stack (docker/docker-compose.yml) reads docker/.env and uses
# DB_PASSWORD to build the prod DATABASE_URL automatically:
#
#     postgresql://coop:${DB_PASSWORD}@db:5432/coop
#
# `db` is the prod Postgres service on the INTERNAL docker network — it has no
# host port. There is no DATABASE_URL to set here; compose constructs it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARD RULES
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  * This is the PRODUCTION env. It must point ONLY at the prod database (the
#    `db` service on the internal docker network). It must NEVER share
#    credentials, a passcode, a session secret, or a database with dev (.env).
#  * docker/.env is gitignored and lives ONLY on this Mac mini. Never commit it.
#  * This *.example template carries NO real secrets and is safe to commit.
#  * DO NOT copy values from .env (dev) into here. Dev is disposable; prod is
#    real life data. Reusing dev's weak "devpassword" in prod defeats the whole
#    point of the two-database split.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SYNTAX TRAPS (compose reads this file)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  * A literal `$` is special: docker compose treats `$word` as a variable and
#    blanks it (you'll see "variable is not set. Defaulting to a blank string"),
#    silently corrupting the value. To use a real `$`, double it: `$$`.
#  * Wrapping a value in double quotes does NOT disable `$` expansion.
#  * DB_PASSWORD is interpolated into a URL (postgresql://coop:<pw>@db:5432/coop),
#    so keep it URL-safe — avoid  $ / @ : ? # %  and spaces. Hex (below) is safe.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_PASSWORD  (REQUIRED, min 16 chars — make it strong AND url-safe)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Password for the prod Postgres `db` service; it ends up inside the prod
# DATABASE_URL, so it must be URL-safe. Hex output (0-9a-f only) avoids every
# URL/compose special character. Generate a fresh value:
#     openssl rand -hex 24
DB_PASSWORD=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_PASSCODE  (REQUIRED)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The real passcode you type to unlock the app. Since Tailscale already keeps
# prod off the public internet, this is what protects prod from other devices
# on your tailnet — choose something only you know, not the dev placeholder.
# NOTE: if your passcode contains a literal `$`, write it as `$$` here (see the
# SYNTAX TRAPS above) or compose will eat part of it.
APP_PASSCODE=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SESSION_SECRET  (REQUIRED)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing secret for session tokens. Generate a fresh long value:
#     openssl rand -base64 48
SESSION_SECRET=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI_API_KEY  (OPTIONAL — leave empty until Sprint 4)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The external AI provider key. Not used until the AI layer lands in Sprint 4;
# leave it empty for now.
AI_API_KEY=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKUP_DIR  (REQUIRED for the nightly backup — Architecture §8, layer 1)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Absolute path on the host where backups are written. On Mac mini, point this
# at an iCloud Drive folder, e.g.
# /Users/<you>/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/coop-backups
#
# The prod compose mounts this into the backup container as /backups
# (volumes: - ${BACKUP_DIR}:/backups). Pointing it at iCloud Drive is what
# gets the dumps offsite automatically. The folder must already exist before
# the backup runs. NOTE: this path contains spaces, which is fine in an env
# file — do NOT add surrounding quotes (compose would treat them literally).
BACKUP_DIR=""
